      "quote": "Autonomic neuropathy affects the nerves that control the heart, regulate blood pressure, and control blood glucose levels. Autonomic neuropathy also affects other internal organs, causing problems with digestion, respiratory function, urination, sexual response, and vision."
    },
    {
      "passage_id": "5_NIDDK_QA:0000034-7#p2",
      "quote": "Nerve damage to the digestive system most commonly causes constipation. Damage can also cause the stomach to empty too slowly, a condition called gastroparesis."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": "The claim that autonomic neuropathy can affect digestion and blood pressure regulation is supported by the text. Passage p1 states that autonomic neuropathy affects nerves that regulate blood pressure and also causes problems with digestion. Passage p2 confirms that nerve damage to the digestive system causes issues such as gastroparesis, which relates to digestion. Both quotes directly support the claim.",
